@@ -1,8 +1,37 @@
 # recordload
-Go 1.22 library. Load reads key=value records in order. Blank lines are ignored.
-The current task adds comment lines beginning with # and reports malformed records
-using an exported *LineError with Line (one-based physical line) and Text fields.
-Load returns the valid prefix on malformed input or read failure. The caller's
-reader errors remain inspectable. Process bytes returned alongside a read error;
-EOF is normal completion. Successful errors are nil interfaces. No line-size cap
-is promised. Empty keys and values are accepted. Readers are borrowed.
+
+Go 1.22 library. `Load(io.Reader) ([]Record, error)` reads newline-separated
+`key=value` records in order. It splits each record at the first `=` and accepts
+empty keys and values. The final line may omit its newline.
+
+Empty lines and comment lines beginning with `#` in column one are ignored,
+including comments containing `=`. Whitespace is preserved: a whitespace-only
+line or an indented comment without `=` is malformed. Line separators are `\n`;
+other bytes, including `\r`, remain part of the line text.
+
+Malformed records produce an exported `*LineError`. Its `Line` field is the
+one-based physical line number, counting blank lines and comments, and its
+`Text` field contains the malformed line without the newline separator. Use
+`errors.As` to inspect it:
+
+```go
+records, err := recordload.Load(reader)
+var lineErr *recordload.LineError
+if errors.As(err, &lineErr) {
+    fmt.Printf("invalid line %d: %q\n", lineErr.Line, lineErr.Text)
+}
+```
+
+On malformed input or a read failure, `Load` returns the valid prefix. It
+processes all bytes delivered by the reader, including bytes delivered alongside
+an error. A final unterminated line is processed by the same rules. `io.EOF` is
+normal completion, and successful calls return a nil error interface.
+
+Reader errors remain available through `errors.Is` and `errors.As`. If the bytes
+read also contain a malformed line, the returned error joins the `*LineError`
+and the reader error, so both remain inspectable. Records after the first
+malformed line are excluded from the result.
+
+Readers are borrowed and never closed. `Load` reads the input into memory; it
+does not impose a fixed line-size limit. The function signature and record
+representation remain unchanged.
